# tools/p/pond_spool_cloth_glow_tend_witness.rish -- Pond's two spool-cloth pedestals, and the
# drift neither can hide.
#
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/p/pond_spool_cloth_glow_tend_witness.rish
#
# WHY THIS RUNG. Tablecloth's four bounds took their desks between 20260829 and 20260830, and the
# last of them left a note on its own placard: pond/apps/spool_cloth.rye took the same wall in the
# same shape on the same day, and its bounds were left to be displayed. The two modules are
# housemates rather than neighbours -- brushstroke/tablecloth.rye, which every Tablecloth desk
# cites, is a symlink into pond/apps/ -- so this is the second half of one room's naming layer
# arriving in the museum, and the first desk that names a pond/apps/ path outright. spool_cloth.rye
# publishes the name bound its Brix-facing manifest and every caller storing a large artifact both
# lean on, and a bound nothing names outside its own source is a bound only its author can check.
#
# WHAT THE DESK SHOWS, AND WHY IT SHOWS TWO THINGS. `max_name = 48` bounds how LONG a large
# artifact's name may be. The manifest grammar needs something else entirely: that a name carry
# neither of the two bytes its line is split on, since the Brix face writes
# `large <name> <whole-hex> <len> <resins>` and reads it back by splitting on newline and then on
# space. Those are different facts and neither follows from the other, which is what REDS %354 cost
# a round to learn one module over. The length was doing the grammar's job by ARITHMETIC -- the
# shortest lawful large line is 76 bytes, so at 48 no name is long enough to carry a forged one --
# and raising the bound for longer human names would have put the forgery back in reach with
# nothing in the number's own comment to say so. So the desk shows the number AND lists the bytes.
#
# WHY THIS DESK CARRIES A READING TABLECLOTH'S DOES NOT. Tablecloth's name desk reads its bound at
# one call site. This module reads `max_name` at the two places a name enters, and only one of them
# is a policy refusal: `store_large` turns away a long name with NameTooLong, and `parse_manifest`
# turns away a long field with BadManifest. The second guards an array's end rather than a policy.
# A parsed entry's name lands in a fixed `[max_name]u8` field, so with that check gone a
# hand-authored manifest carrying a sixty-byte name is copied past the end of the array -- a
# refusal downgraded to a panic. A pedestal claiming the bound is wired while reading one of its
# two call sites would say something true about half the module.
#
# The two edges are read as two readings rather than one because they fire apart: delete either
# call and the other still stands, which the control proves in both directions rather than
# asserting. Two readings that always fire together would be one reading wearing two names
# (.claude/rules/derived-spine.md), and these are not that.
#
# WHAT IS PROVEN, on metal.
#   The placard reads in the order src/shape/PLACARD.md seats: name, shape, invariant, example,
#   readers, nib.
#   The pedestal names the Rye file its number and its wall come from.
#   The number the pedestal shows EQUALS `max_name` as pond/apps/spool_cloth.rye publishes it. The
#   scan reads both sides and compares; it carries no value of its own, so raising the bound
#   honestly in both rooms leaves the guard quiet -- proven as a welcome rather than assumed.
#   The desk agrees with ITSELF: the number in its `shape` line equals the number under `example`,
#   so a bound desk's two spellings cannot part inside one file.
#   The pedestal lists the bytes `name_is_one_field` refuses, compared as sorted sets, so a third
#   byte joining the wall in the Rye alone reds here while every length still agrees.
#   `store_large` still consults that wall, and `parse_manifest` still reads that bound, so the
#   invariant the desk displays stays wired at both edges rather than merely written.
#   The nib the desk says it displays at equals the version the module publishes. PLACARD.md seats
#   six lines and gives the sixth its reason -- a pedestal always displays a value AT a nib -- and
#   every placard reading in the tree counts that KEYWORD and stops, so across the 37 residents
#   standing before this desk the stamp after it is compared to nothing. A module whose version
#   moves while its desk keeps the elder nib is a desk saying it shows a value from a release the
#   module has left, and an honest bump moves both, proven as a welcome.
#   The desk lowers through Glow, builds through the Zig toolchain, and runs to EXIT:0.
#   Every reading refuses from the other side: forty plantings in a throwaway pen, each caught by
#   the reading it was planted against, seven honest trees walking free, and thirteen named readings
#   checked while a neighbour refuses -- which is how each set of edges is shown to be several.
#
# AND THE SECOND PEDESTAL, seated 20260831: max_large_artifacts, the catalog's seat count, shown
# beside a number this module never spells. The shared BeadStore is a second wall over the same
# room, and it counts BYTES where the catalog counts NAMES -- so a caller storing ceiling-sized
# artifacts meets StoreFull with seats still free. Measured on metal by the module's own selftest:
# two ceiling artifacts held 256 of 256 beads, and the next refused with two of four seats free.
#
# That guarantee is DERIVED two levels deep across two modules -- max_store_beads divided by
# max_resins times (max_resin_bytes over max_bead_bytes) -- so the scan RECOMPUTES it out of
# mantra/beading.rye and mantra/spool.rye rather than reading a copy, and reads the module's own
# derivation as a fact of its own: respell it as a literal 2 and every number still agrees while
# the sentence has gone false.
#
# THE READING NO DESK IN THIS ROOM HAS CARRIED. The bound is the length of THREE fixed arrays in
# TWO modules -- LargeCatalog.entries and Manifest.entries here, and KeyedLargeCatalog.owners in
# pond/apps/spool_keyed.rye. Two walls stand in front of them, and the third array has none of its
# own: store_owned_large writes owners[before] after store_large returns, riding on that refusal
# through a `try`. So the bound is read where it CROSSES a module boundary, and the control proves
# the three fire apart by reading each survivor by name while its neighbour refuses.
#
# The housemate next door needs none of this, checked rather than assumed: Tablecloth seats 32
# artifacts of one resin each, which costs 64 of the store's 256 beads, so every seat is reachable.
# A spool artifact spans 64 resins, and that is the whole difference.
#
# WHERE A ROSTER DOES LIVE, said plainly, because the sentence above is true of the scan and not of
# this file. This witness PINS one list the scan deliberately declines to hold -- the two reserved
# bytes -- so widening the wall reds here even where the scan welcomes it. That is a pin rather
# than a comparison, and it is deliberate: the alphabet a store forbids is a published surface, and
# moving it is worth a hand's eye. The cost is one line edited here on an honest change, and the
# reason is written down so the next hand meets a review gate rather than a mystery.
#
# WHAT IS NOT PROVEN. Whether forty-eight is the right name length or four the right number of
# seats, which are Pond's own design questions, and whether the arithmetic that makes 48 safe still
# holds if the manifest grammar changes -- a pedestal displays what the source decided. And any
# behavior of the catalog itself, which pond/apps/spool_cloth.rye's own selftest holds on real
# bytes. The eight ClothError paths are this module's remaining bound and want their own desk.

say "spool-cloth-tend: Language EN -- the spool binds a name to a whole artifact, and both the name's bound and the catalog's capacity now stand in the shape museum beside their housemate's."
say "spool-cloth-tend: Style Gauge -- the placard reads in the museum's seated order, and the number it shows is compared to the source rather than remembered."
say "spool-cloth-tend: Lens TAME -- bound everything and say why: the module names its maxima, the pedestals name them, and this proves each is one number read at every edge that reads it -- two for the name, three for the seats, across two modules."

say "spool-cloth-tend: the pedestal against its source..."
let scan = run ["sh" "tools/fixtures/p/pond_spool_cloth_glow_tend_scan.sh"]
assert scan.ok else "spool-cloth-tend: the pedestal and its source disagree -- read the verdict line"
say scan.out
assert scan.out contains "verdict=agree" else "spool-cloth-tend: the pedestal and its source must name one number"
assert scan.out contains "placard_order=name shape invariant example readers nib" else "spool-cloth-tend: the placard's six lines keep their seated order"
assert scan.out contains "citation=yes" else "spool-cloth-tend: the pedestal names the source its number and its wall come from"
assert scan.out contains "desk_bound=48" else "spool-cloth-tend: the desk's shape line and its example are one number"
assert scan.out contains "store_wired=yes" else "spool-cloth-tend: store_large must still consult name_is_one_field, or the desk's invariant is only written"
assert scan.out contains "parse_wired=yes" else "spool-cloth-tend: parse_manifest must still read max_name, or a manifest line copies past the end of a fixed field"
say "field GREEN -- the pedestal stands on what its source publishes, at both edges the bound is read"
say "spool-cloth-tend: the capacity pedestal -- seats, and the bytes behind them..."
assert scan.out contains "capacity_placard_order=name shape invariant example readers nib" else "spool-cloth-tend: the capacity placard's six lines keep their seated order"
assert scan.out contains "capacity_citation=yes" else "spool-cloth-tend: the capacity desk names all four rooms its number and its guarantee come from"
assert scan.out contains "capacity_desk_bound=4" else "spool-cloth-tend: the capacity desk's shape line and its example are one number"
assert scan.out contains "capacity_store_wired=yes" else "spool-cloth-tend: store_large must still refuse a full catalog, or two arrays in two modules are written past their end"
assert scan.out contains "capacity_parse_wired=yes" else "spool-cloth-tend: parse_manifest must still refuse an overlong manifest"
assert scan.out contains "capacity_keyed_wired=yes" else "spool-cloth-tend: spool_keyed must still size its owners array by this bound rather than by a literal of its own"
assert scan.out contains "capacity_derivation_wired=yes" else "spool-cloth-tend: the guarantee must stay derived from the store rather than spelled"
say "capacity GREEN -- four seats stand beside the ceiling artifacts the store can pay for, and the bound is wired at all three arrays"

say "spool-cloth-tend: the nib -- the placard's sixth line, read rather than trusted..."
assert scan.out contains "verdict=agree" else "spool-cloth-tend: the desk's nib and the module's version must be one stamp"
say "nib GREEN -- the desk displays its value at the version the module publishes"

say "spool-cloth-tend: the pinned alphabet -- a review gate, not a comparison (see the header)..."
assert scan.out contains "desk_bytes=newline space" else "spool-cloth-tend: the two reserved bytes are PINNED here; widening the wall is a review gate, so move this line and its twin"
assert scan.out contains "rye_wall_bytes=newline space" else "spool-cloth-tend: the wall's own bytes are pinned beside the desk's, so both move together or neither does"
say "pin GREEN -- the forbidden alphabet stands where it stood"

say "spool-cloth-tend: the control, both ways..."
let control = run ["sh" "tools/fixtures/p/pond_spool_cloth_glow_tend_control.sh"]
assert control.ok else "spool-cloth-tend: the control must complete"
say control.out
assert control.out contains "welcomes=7" else "spool-cloth-tend: an honest tree, two honest raises, an honestly widened wall, an honest store raise, and an honest version bump across BOTH desks must all walk free"
assert control.out contains "refusals=40" else "spool-cloth-tend: every reading must refuse from its own side"
assert control.out contains "readings=13" else "spool-cloth-tend: each edge must be read while its neighbour refuses, or the three are one reading wearing three names"
assert control.out contains "wrong=0" else "spool-cloth-tend: no case may land on the side it was not planted for"
assert control.out contains "control_verdict=ok" else "spool-cloth-tend: the control must agree with itself"
say "RED and GREEN paths proven -- forty plantings refused, seven honest trees welcomed, thirteen readings held"

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let desk = "src/shape/shape-spool-cloth-name-bound.glow"
let capacity_desk = "src/shape/shape-spool-cloth-catalog-capacity.glow"

say "spool-cloth-tend: lower, build, run the name-bound pedestal..."
let gate = run ["env" "RYE_ZIG=${zig}" "sh" "tools/g/glow_run_worker.sh" desk]
assert gate.ok else "spool-cloth-tend: the name-bound pedestal must lower, build, and run"
assert gate.out contains "EXIT:0" else "spool-cloth-tend: the name-bound pedestal did not exit 0"
say "metal GREEN -- the desk lowers through Glow, builds through Zig, and runs"

say "spool-cloth-tend: lower, build, run the catalog-capacity pedestal..."
let cap_gate = run ["env" "RYE_ZIG=${zig}" "sh" "tools/g/glow_run_worker.sh" capacity_desk]
assert cap_gate.ok else "spool-cloth-tend: the catalog-capacity pedestal must lower, build, and run"
assert cap_gate.out contains "EXIT:0" else "spool-cloth-tend: the catalog-capacity pedestal did not exit 0"
say "metal GREEN -- both pedestals lower through Glow, build through Zig, and run"

say "GREEN: spool-cloth Glow Tend -- two pedestals stand. max_name shows the alphabet its wall removes beside it and is proven wired at both the store edge and the manifest edge; max_large_artifacts shows the ceiling artifacts its shared store can pay for, recomputed out of Mantra rather than copied, and proven wired at all three arrays the bound sizes across two modules."
